    sections: [
      { title: 'Model a resource and its owner', body: `Design an API around the operation a client needs rather than exposing a database table unchanged. A document has an identifier, an owner or tenant, a version, and a set of permitted actions. A create endpoint should validate both the shape of the submitted data and the caller's right to create it in the selected workspace. A read endpoint must verify access to the requested object.

Avoid trusting a tenant identifier merely because it arrived in a request body. Derive the permitted tenant scope from authenticated membership or another server-verified relationship. Unpredictable identifiers reduce casual guessing but do not establish permission. Describe the authorization decision alongside the resource contract rather than leaving it for a later “security pass.”` },
      { title: 'Define the retry contract', body: `A timeout tells the client that it did not receive a timely result; it does not prove that the server did nothing. For a create operation with a costly side effect, accept an operation key and associate it with the authenticated owner, request parameters, and durable result. Repeating the same logical request should retrieve the recorded outcome instead of initiating another side effect.

The claim needs an atomic boundary. If two requests can both observe a missing key and then execute, the deduplication mechanism races. Define how concurrent attempts are serialized, how changed parameters are rejected, and how an incomplete operation is recovered. Also state retention: reusing a key after its record expires may create a new operation. Do not promise an unlimited guarantee from a short-lived cache.` },
      { title: 'Protect edits with a version', body: `For an editable resource, a version or entity tag can identify the state on which the client based its changes. The update includes that precondition. The authoritative store applies the change only if the stored version still matches, and increments the version atomically. Otherwise it returns a conflict or failed-precondition response that lets the client reconcile deliberately.

This differs from idempotency. A version protects against overwriting someone else's newer edit; an operation key recognizes a retry of the same operation. A robust API may need both. Explain what the client does after a conflict, including preserving its local draft. Automatically retrying an old replacement body without re-reading and reconciling can reintroduce the lost-update problem.` },
      { title: 'Make pagination stable and bounded', body: `Choose an ordering key that is deterministic, such as creation time plus a unique identifier. A cursor records the last observed position, and the next request asks for records after that position. The unique tie-breaker matters when multiple rows share a timestamp. Limit page size and validate the cursor rather than permitting an arbitrarily expensive query.

Cursor pagination avoids the shifting offsets caused by inserting rows before a numbered page, but it does not automatically create a historical snapshot. Updates, deletions, and changes in permissions can still alter the result set. If the product requires a repeatable export, describe a snapshot boundary or versioned view separately. Bind cursor scope to the intended tenant, filters, and ordering contract.` },
      { title: 'Expose outcomes clients can act on', body: `Errors should distinguish malformed input, missing permission, resource conflict, dependency unavailability, and rate limits without leaking private details. Decide whether a forbidden object should appear nonexistent to unauthorized callers. Include retry guidance only when retry is safe and useful, and make timeout budgets visible across downstream calls.

Test the contract using failures as well as successful requests: repeat an operation key, change its parameters, issue competing versioned updates, expire a cursor, and retry after the response is lost. Keep provider-specific behavior scoped to that provider. For example, Stripe documents how its own idempotency layer records results; another service must explicitly define its policy rather than inheriting that behavior by vocabulary alone.` },
    ],
    exercise: 'Design create, list, and versioned-update endpoints for a team document service. Specify tenant authorization, idempotency retention, cursor ordering, and the response to two competing edits.',
